-- Behaviour-oriented concurrency (Cheeseman et al., OOPSLA 2023), the
-- runtime's own effect: a cown holds a value; `when c body` spawns a
-- behaviour, which later, once, with every cown of `c` its own at once,
-- is given what they hold and gives back what they are to hold after
-- it.  Behaviours whose cowns overlap run one after another, in the
-- order they were spawned; the others run at once, on as many threads
-- as the machine has cores.  A behaviour takes its cowns all at once,
-- never one and then another, so behaviours do not deadlock on them.
-- Import it by a name, `import boc as BOC`, and name `BOC.boc` in the
-- program's world.
--
-- > [!CAUTION]
-- > Unstable: concurrency in heff is not settled. What this module offers
-- > may change, or go, in any release.
--
-- ```haskell
-- import boc as BOC
-- import co
-- import std.io
--
-- -- | `n` moved from one account to the other: one behaviour on both
-- paid : {e : HFunctor}
--      -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
--      -> BOC.Cown Int
--      -> BOC.Cown Int
--      -> Int
--      -> Co e Unit
-- paid from to n = do
--   with x := from
--        y := to do
--     x := x - n
--     y := y + n
--
-- -- | alice given 10, then 5 paid to bob; what bob has then, 5, said
-- -- | by a last behaviour on bob, which comes after the others on him
-- main : Co (BOC.boc <+> stdio) Unit
-- main = do
--   alice <- BOC.newCown 0
--   bob <- BOC.newCown 0
--   with a := alice do
--     a := a + 10
--   paid alice bob 5
--   with b := bob do
--     print (showInt b)
-- ```
--
-- Operation | What it does
-- --- | ---
-- `newCown v` | a cown holding `v`
-- `when c body` | a behaviour spawned on the cown `c`, or a tuple of them; this one goes on
-- `with a := c do ...` | the same, its body statements, `a` what `c` holds (more cowns, a binding each)
-- `with a := c once p do ...` | the same, its body run once `p` holds of what the cowns hold: until then, the behaviour waits for one `p` reads to be written
-- `with r := BOC.await act do ...` | `act`, of the world, run outside every behaviour; the body a behaviour once it has come to `r`; this one goes on
-- `with a := c` / `r := BOC.await act do ...` | the same, the body a behaviour on `c` once `act` has come to `r`
-- `with r := BOC.awaitFirst a b do ...` | `a` and `b` raced: the body once the first has come to its value, `r` `Left` or `Right` it, the other given up
-- `parMap f xs k` | `f` of each of `xs`, each in a behaviour of its own, at once; `k` given them, in order, by a behaviour once all are done
-- `workers` | how many threads the program's pool runs behaviours on
--
-- A behaviour's body is given what its cowns hold, a tuple as the cowns
-- are (`(a, b, log)` hands it an `(x, y, l)`), and gives back their new
-- contents, the same shape: `Cowns` says what a cown or a tuple of them
-- holds.  A `with` of cowns is a behaviour too, each cown bound to a
-- name (`with x := from` and `y := to` under it), its statements the
-- body, the names what they hold, set to change it: only the cowns whose
-- names it sets are written back, and the `with` comes to `()`.  A
-- `with` of cowns with a condition, `with a := c once p do ...`, is a
-- behaviour that, once it has its cowns, checks `p` of what they hold:
-- where it does not hold, the behaviour lets go of them, untouched, and
-- waits until a behaviour writes a cown whose name `p` reads; then it is
-- queued again on all of them, behind that one, and checks `p` again
-- (the paper's `whenever`).  One still waiting once every other
-- behaviour of the program is done is dropped, its body never run.
-- The same cown twice in one `when` or `with` -- two names bound to one
-- cown, each written back, would leave no answer which is kept -- is a
-- failure, found as the behaviour is spawned: said as a body's is,
-- with where among its cowns the two are (its 1st and 2nd, say), the
-- behaviour never queued, the one spawning it going on.  Where two
-- cowns may be one, the program tells them apart first.
--
-- A body is a computation of the world the `when` is in (the `with`'s),
-- held by the operation and wrapped, as an operation's computations are
-- (`hmap`), by the handler in force there.  Where that is the program's
-- entry, the runtime's, the body is compiled with it known, and the
-- operations in it are handed to their providers where they are, as
-- the `when`'s own is: a body may print, spawn behaviours, and do what
-- else the program's world does, whose providers must then be safe on
-- threads.  A handler between the `when` and the program's world maps
-- the body as it maps any computation an operation of its world holds,
-- where the `when` passes it, not where the body runs: what the body
-- does there, it does as that handler made of it then.  The body runs
-- later, once, on whichever thread takes it: what it needs that it
-- cannot do in the world, it is given as a value.
--
-- So a behaviour runs before every behaviour spawned after it on a cown
-- they share, wherever in the source it is.  There is no barrier, and
-- nothing waits for behaviours to come back with a value: a behaviour
-- that is to come after others on a cown is spawned after them on it,
-- and what they came to is given on, to the behaviours after them
-- (`parMap`'s `k`).
--
-- The program is the scope: a program whose world has `boc` has one
-- pool, of as many threads as the machine has cores, opened by its
-- first operation of boc's; `main` spawns in it, and so does every
-- body.  The program ends once `main` has come to its end and no
-- behaviour is queued or runs and no act is pending -- those still
-- waiting on a condition dropped then.  So a server ends once nothing
-- awaits its listener any more: its listener closed (std.net's
-- `closeListener`), or the custodian it was awaited in shut down.
--
-- A behaviour runs from its start to its end and never waits on the
-- world: what it would wait for -- a connection accepted, a socket
-- read, a sleep -- it awaits, as Verona's behaviours do, by a `with`
-- whose last binding is `r := BOC.await act`.  The `with` waits for
-- nothing, and the behaviour goes on: `act`, a computation of the
-- world the `with` is in, runs outside every behaviour, and once it
-- has come to its value, the body is a behaviour of its own, `r` that
-- value.  With cowns bound before it (`with x := c` and `r := BOC.await
-- act` under it; several cowns, a tuple of them to one name, `x := (c0,
-- c1)`), it is a behaviour on them, spawned only then: no cown is held
-- while the act waits, and behaviours on a cown that await acts come in
-- the order their acts came to their values.  What an act fails at is
-- its value, for the body to handle -- `Net.read`'s `Either NetError
-- Bytes`, a time limit its own (`Net.read (timeout := 5000) c n`); an
-- act that itself fails -- abandons, or fails in the runtime -- is as a
-- body that fails: `failed` is given why, and the `with`'s body never
-- runs.  `awaitFirst a b` starts both; the first to come to its value
-- gives up the other, and the body is a behaviour on its value alone.
-- An act is of the `with`'s world, whatever world that is -- a
-- concrete `main`'s, or one a handler adds to, a `recover`'s, an
-- `attempt`'s: the `with` says so, and the act needs no annotation.
--
-- ```haskell
-- import boc as BOC
-- import co
-- import std.duration as Duration
-- import std.time
--
-- -- | a second's sleep awaited, then 1 added to the count, by a
-- -- | behaviour on it: the count is held by none meanwhile
-- ticked : {e : HFunctor}
--        -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
--        -> {{clock : time <: e}}
--        -> BOC.Cown Int
--        -> Co e Unit
-- ticked count = do
--   with n := count
--        _ := BOC.await (sleep (Duration.seconds 1)) do
--     n := n + 1
-- ```
--
-- A behaviour, and an act, is a user of a custodian, not one of its
-- things: it takes the custodian current where it is spawned or
-- awaited, and runs with that one current, so the things its `with`s
-- hold are in its custody.  An act awaited is one of that custodian's
-- things besides: where the custodian is shut down while the act waits,
-- the act is given up -- its wait undone, what comes after it let go
-- of -- and the behaviour after it is never spawned.  So a server is
-- stopped by shutting down the custody its acts were awaited in: the
-- act waiting for a connection let go of, its listener closed, and the
-- program ends once the behaviours left are done.  One that has come to
-- its value is not given up.  No shutdown stops a behaviour.
--
-- A behaviour whose body fails -- abandons, panics (`panic why`), or
-- fails in the runtime -- is done with that: its cowns hold what they
-- held, since what they are to hold is written after the body, and are
-- handed on as if it had ended.  An act that fails is the same, and the
-- behaviour after it is never spawned.  Each failure is said on stderr,
-- as the program's own is (`heff: why`), and the others go on: the
-- program ends as it would, by 1.  What a caller is to handle is an
-- `exception`'s throw, caught in the body (`attempt`), not a failure.

-- The runtime's side (boc.provider.c): each cown is a turnstile, the
-- last request queued at it, and the value it holds.  A behaviour is
-- queued at each of its cowns' in two phases, by swaps, in the order of
-- their addresses, then runs on a thread of the pool once it is first
-- at each, and lets the next through at each as it ends.  No lock but
-- the pool's own, around the behaviours ready.  A behaviour readied by
-- one a thread runs -- spawned, or handed a cown -- is kept by that
-- thread and run next there, nothing woken, until it has run 64 in a row
-- with others ready.  A cown's value is the cown's, read by the
-- behaviour holding it as its own (owned, not shared): an array a cown
-- holds a body writes in place, and the runtime puts back what it wrote
-- where the body fails or does not write the cown.  The behaviours
-- waiting on a cown to be written (`once`) are a list beside it, the
-- holder's to read and write.
--
-- Each thread of a pool waits on the world through a group of the
-- runtime's reactor of its own (std.reactor's).  An act awaited by a
-- behaviour a thread runs is that thread's, started there once the
-- behaviour is done, run until it is done or until an operation of it
-- would wait (a read, a sleep), which is then pending in the thread's
-- reactor, the rest of the act kept with it.  So the act's rest, which
-- spawns the behaviour after it, runs on that thread, and that
-- behaviour is the one it runs next: no other thread between an act and
-- its behaviour.  An act awaited by `main`, outside every behaviour, is
-- a behaviour on no cown first, whose thread takes it.  A thread with
-- nothing to run waits in its reactor where acts of its are pending --
-- woken there where a behaviour is ready -- and looks at it, not
-- waiting, every 64 behaviours.  The program's thread, once `main` has
-- come to its end, waits until no behaviour is queued or runs and no act
-- is pending, then stops the pool's threads: a server whose listener is
-- closed ends once its connections are done.  An act in a
-- custody is in a group of its own within its thread's, which a thing
-- the custodian has gives up (std.reactor's `giving`); the two acts of
-- a race are each in one, the first to come to its value closing the
-- other's.
--
-- A behaviour is never stopped partway -- its body runs to its end,
-- holding its cowns -- so the one a thread keeps would wait for the end
-- of the body it runs, however long that goes on.  The thread counts
-- the body's operations of boc's, and at every 256th puts the behaviour
-- it keeps with the others ready, which wakes a thread to take it.  A
-- body that computes long doing none holds its thread, and the one
-- kept, until it ends; that is the program's to avoid.
--
-- JavaScript's (boc.provider.js): one thread, the behaviours run in
-- turns, each once it is first at every cown it is queued at, in the
-- order they were readied.  A body whose rest comes later (a sleep, an
-- operation of another provider that waits) holds its cowns until then.
-- An act awaited is a turn of its own, holding no cown; of two raced,
-- the other is not given up, nothing there undoing a wait, but runs on,
-- its rest doing nothing; nor is an act given up by a custodian.

module boc
  ( Cown
  , Cowns
  , instanceCownsCown
  , instanceCownsPair
  , instanceBinderCown
  , instanceBinderCowns
  , instanceBinderCownsMore
  , instanceHolderCown
  , instanceHolderCowns
  , instanceHolderCownsMore
  , instanceWaiterCown
  , instanceWaiterCowns
  , instanceWaiterCownsMore
  , Await
  , await
  , awaitFirst
  , instanceBinderAwait
  , instanceBinderCownsAwait
  , instanceHolderAwait
  , instanceHolderCownsAwait
  , boc
  , newCown
  , workers
  , when
  , parMap
  )

import array as Array
import co

-- Cowns
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | A cown holding an `a`.  (The runtime's own, a foreign value: no
-- | value of the program has it.)
data Cown a

-- (a `Bool` of a `with`'s names, the first's, or `True` where none is)
firstFlag : List Bool -> Bool
firstFlag (b :: rest) = b
firstFlag [] = True

-- (a pair's `Bool`s a cown, `l` and `r` cowns on its sides, from a
-- `Bool` a name: the last name the whole pair's, else the first the
-- left side's and the rest, by `rest`, the right side's)
spreadPair : Int -> Int -> (List Bool -> List Bool) -> List Bool -> List Bool
spreadPair l r rest [b] = List.replicate (l + r) b
spreadPair l r rest (b :: bs) = List.replicate l b ++ rest bs
spreadPair l r rest [] = List.replicate (l + r) True

-- | What a behaviour can be on: a cown, or a tuple of them (`(a, b, c)`),
-- | what they hold, a tuple as they are, and how many cowns.  The
-- | runtime reads what they hold as the tuple says, and writes it back,
-- | a cown where its `Bool` says, in order.  `spread` makes those `Bool`s
-- | from a `with`'s, one a name: its names bound along the pairs to the
-- | right (`with a := c0`, `b := (c1, c2)` and `c := c3` binds `(c0,
-- | ((c1, c2), c3))`), the last to what is left, each name's `Bool` for
-- | every cown it holds.
theory Cowns (c : Type) where
  Contents : Type
  width : Int
  spread : List Bool -> List Bool

-- | A cown: what it holds.
instance instanceCownsCown : {a : Type} -> Cowns (Cown a)
instanceCownsCown = { Contents = a, width = 1, spread = \fs -> [firstFlag fs] }

-- | Two, each a cown or a tuple: the pair of what they hold.
instance instanceCownsPair : {c d : Type}
                           -> {{l : Cowns c}}
                           -> {{r : Cowns d}}
                           -> Cowns (c, d)
instanceCownsPair =
  { Contents = (l.Contents, r.Contents)
  , width = l.width + r.width
  , spread = spreadPair l.width r.width r.spread
  }

-- The effect
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | A value of some type, the type hidden: what an operation of a type
-- | of its own holds (the effect has no parameter for it).
type Some (f : Type -> Type) = (r : Type) -> ((a : Type) -> f a -> r) -> r

-- | ... of two types.
type Some2 (f : Type -> Type -> Type) =
  (r : Type) -> ((a b : Type) -> f a b -> r) -> r

-- (what it holds, changed, of whatever type)
someThen : {f g : Type -> Type}
         -> ({a : Type} -> f a -> g a)
         -> Some f
         -> Some g
someThen h s = \r use -> s r (\a v -> use a (h v))

-- (a cown made: what it holds, and what comes after it)
data Cowning x a = MkCowning a (Cown a -> x)

-- (a behaviour asked for: its cowns, a `c`, a cown or a tuple of them;
-- the condition its body waits for, of what they hold, a `t`, where it
-- has one; and which cowns its body writes back, and which its
-- condition reads, a bit each, in the order of the tuple, all where
-- -1)
data Behaviour c t = MkBehaviour c (Maybe (t -> Bool)) Int Int

-- | A behaviour running, or an act, as the runtime knows it: what its body takes and gives is its.  (The runtime's own: no
-- | value of the program has it.)
data Turn

-- (what the cowns of the behaviour running hold, taken; and what they
-- are to hold, given)
data Taking x t = MkTaking Turn (t -> x)

data Giving x t = MkGiving Turn t x

cowningThen : {x y a : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Cowning x a -> Cowning y a
cowningThen g (MkCowning v k) = MkCowning v (\c -> g (k c))

takingThen : {x y t : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Taking x t -> Taking y t
takingThen g (MkTaking tn k) = MkTaking tn (\v -> g (k v))

givingThen : {x y t : Type} -> (x -> y) -> Giving x t -> Giving y t
givingThen g (MkGiving tn v x) = MkGiving tn v (g x)

-- | An operation of `boc`: a cown made; a behaviour spawned, its body a
-- | computation of `m`'s, given its turn, that takes what its cowns hold
-- | (`Taken`) and gives what they are to hold (`Given`); an act awaited, run
-- | outside every behaviour, what comes once it has its value -- the
-- | behaviour after it spawned -- its own rest; two acts raced, each
-- | given the race as its turn, and asking it, once it has its value,
-- | whether it is the first to (`Won`), the other given up where it is;
-- | how many threads the pool runs behaviours on (`Workers`).  The
-- | computations are `m`'s, wrapped where the operation is, by the
-- | handler in force there; what is of a type of its own is beside them,
-- | not mapped.
data BocF (m : Type -> Type) x
  = NewCown (Some (Cowning x))
  | When (Some2 Behaviour) (Turn -> m Unit) x
  | Taken (Some (Taking x))
  | Given (Some (Giving x))
  | Awaits (Turn -> m Unit) x
  | Races (Turn -> m Unit) (Turn -> m Unit) x
  | Won Turn (Bool -> x)
  | Workers (Int -> x)

-- | Behaviours on cowns, the runtime's: cowns made, behaviours queued on
-- | them, each run once its cowns are free, on the program's pool.
boc : HFunctor
boc =
  { f = BocF
  , hfmap = \m g p -> case p of
      NewCown s -> NewCown (someThen (cowningThen g) s)
      When s b x -> When s b (g x)
      Taken s -> Taken (someThen (takingThen g) s)
      Given s -> Given (someThen (givingThen g) s)
      Awaits c x -> Awaits c (g x)
      Races l r x -> Races l r (g x)
      Won tn k -> Won tn (\b -> g (k b))
      Workers k -> Workers (\n -> g (k n))
  , hmap = \m n nt p -> case p of
      NewCown s -> NewCown s
      When s b x -> When s (\tn -> nt (b tn)) x
      Taken s -> Taken s
      Given s -> Given s
      Awaits c x -> Awaits (\tn -> nt (c tn)) x
      Races l r x -> Races (\tn -> nt (l tn)) (\tn -> nt (r tn)) x
      Won tn k -> Won tn k
      Workers k -> Workers k
  }

-- | A cown holding `v`.
newCown : {e : HFunctor}
        -> {a : Type}
        -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
        -> a
        -> Co e (Cown a)
newCown v = op (bc (NewCown (\r use -> use a (MkCowning v (\c -> c))))) val

-- | How many threads the program's pool runs behaviours on, at least
-- | 1: as many as the machine has cores, on C's; 1 in JavaScript's one
-- | thread.  Asked before the pool is opened, it is how many it will
-- | have; asking does not open it.  So work split in as many parts as
-- | there are workers keeps each busy, and no more parts than that.
workers : {e : HFunctor} -> {{bc : boc <: e}} -> Co e Int
workers = op (bc (Workers (\n -> n))) val

-- (what the cowns of the behaviour running, `tn`, hold, as the runtime
-- took them for its body: of the type the body takes, which the
-- runtime gives the one `when` asked for)
taken : {e : HFunctor} -> {t : Type} -> {{bc : boc <: e}} -> Turn -> Co e t
taken tn = op (bc (Taken (\r use -> use t (MkTaking tn (\v -> v))))) val

-- (... what they are to hold after it, for the runtime to write back)
given : {e : HFunctor}
      -> {t : Type}
      -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
      -> Turn
      -> t
      -> Co e Unit
given tn v = op (bc (Given (\r use -> use t (MkGiving tn v MkUnit)))) val

-- (a body, as the runtime runs it in its turn: given what the cowns
-- hold, and what it comes to given back)
ran : {e : HFunctor}
    -> {t : Type}
    -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
    -> (t -> Co e t)
    -> Turn
    -> Co e Unit
ran body tn = bind (taken tn) (\v -> bind (body v) (\w -> given tn w))

-- (the `Bool`s a bit each, the first the lowest)
bitsOf : List Bool -> Int
bitsOf [] = 0
bitsOf (b :: bs) = (if b then 1 else 0) + 2 * bitsOf bs

-- (a behaviour on `cs`, its body run once `ok`, where it is given,
-- holds of what they hold, waiting meanwhile on the cowns of the names
-- `read` says, and writing back those of the names `written` says: a
-- `Bool` a name of a `with`, spread over its cowns)
behaviourWhere : {c : Type}
               -> {e : HFunctor}
               -> c
               -> {{cw : Cowns c}}
               -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
               -> List Bool
               -> List Bool
               -> Maybe (cw.Contents -> Bool)
               -> (cw.Contents -> Co e cw.Contents)
               -> Co e Unit
behaviourWhere cs written read ok body =
  op
    (bc
       (When
          (\r use ->
             use
               c
               cw.Contents
               (MkBehaviour
                  cs
                  ok
                  (bitsOf (cw.spread written))
                  (bitsOf (cw.spread read))))
          (ran body)
          MkUnit))
    val

-- (a behaviour on `cs`, writing back the cowns `written` says)
behaviour : {c : Type}
          -> {e : HFunctor}
          -> c
          -> {{cw : Cowns c}}
          -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
          -> List Bool
          -> (cw.Contents -> Co e cw.Contents)
          -> Co e Unit
behaviour cs written body =
  behaviourWhere cs (cw := cw) written Nil Nothing body

-- | A behaviour on the cowns `cs`, one or a tuple of them, spawned;
-- | this one goes on.  Its turn comes once every behaviour spawned
-- | before it on any of them is done; then `body` is given what they
-- | hold, a tuple as `cs` is, and what it gives back is what they hold
-- | after.  The body is a computation of this world, run later, on
-- | whichever thread takes it.  The same cown twice is a failure, the
-- | behaviour not spawned.
when : {c : Type}
     -> {e : HFunctor}
     -> c
     -> {{cw : Cowns c}}
     -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
     -> (cw.Contents -> Co e cw.Contents)
     -> Co e Unit
when cs body =
  op
    (bc
       (When
          (\r use -> use c cw.Contents (MkBehaviour cs Nothing (0 - 1) 0))
          (ran body)
          MkUnit))
    val

-- The `with` of cowns
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | A `with` of a cown: a behaviour on it, spawned (`with a := c do
-- | ...`: `a` what it holds); this one goes on, and the `with` comes to
-- | nothing.  Its body is of the world of the block around.
instance instanceBinderCown : {a : Type} -> Binder (Cown a)
instanceBinderCown = { Contents = \x s -> a, Result = \r -> Unit }

-- | A `with` of two cowns, each bound to a name (`with a := c0` and `b :=
-- | c1` under it): one behaviour on both, which takes them at once.
instance instanceBinderCowns : {a b : Type}
                             -> {{cw : Cowns (Cown a, Cown b)}}
                             -> Binder (Cown a, Cown b)
instanceBinderCowns = { Contents = \x s -> cw.Contents, Result = \r -> Unit }

-- | ... of more cowns (`with a := c0`, `b := c1` and `c := c2`): one
-- | behaviour on all of them, which takes them all at once.
instance instanceBinderCownsMore : {a d f : Type}
                                 -> {{cw : Cowns (Cown a, (d, f))}}
                                 -> Binder (Cown a, (d, f))
instanceBinderCownsMore =
  { Contents = \x s -> cw.Contents, Result = \r -> Unit }

-- | A behaviour on a cown held by a `with`: its body's names set are
-- | what the cown holds after it; one only read is not written back.
instance instanceHolderCown : {e : HFunctor}
                            -> {a : Type}
                            -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
                            -> Holder
                              e
                              (Cown a)
                              (\s -> a)
                              (\x -> x)
                              (\r -> Unit)
instanceHolderCown =
  { held = \c written body ->
        behaviour
          c
          written
          (\v -> bind (body (s := Unit) v) (\p -> pure (snd p)))
  }

-- | ... on two cowns.
instance instanceHolderCowns : {e : HFunctor}
                             -> {a b : Type}
                             -> {{cw : Cowns (Cown a, Cown b)}}
                             -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
                             -> Holder
                               e
                               (Cown a, Cown b)
                               (\s -> cw.Contents)
                               (\x -> x)
                               (\r -> Unit)
instanceHolderCowns =
  { held = \c written body ->
        behaviour
          c
          (cw := cw)
          written
          (\v -> bind (body (s := Unit) v) (\p -> pure (snd p)))
  }

-- | ... on more.
instance instanceHolderCownsMore : {e : HFunctor}
                                 -> {a d f : Type}
                                 -> {{cw : Cowns (Cown a, (d, f))}}
                                 -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
                                 -> Holder
                                   e
                                   (Cown a, (d, f))
                                   (\s -> cw.Contents)
                                   (\x -> x)
                                   (\r -> Unit)
instanceHolderCownsMore =
  { held = \c written body ->
        behaviour
          c
          (cw := cw)
          written
          (\v -> bind (body (s := Unit) v) (\p -> pure (snd p)))
  }

-- | A behaviour on a cown held by a `with` with a condition, `with a :=
-- | c once p do ...`: once it has the cown, `p` checked of what it
-- | holds; where it does not hold, the cown let go of, untouched, and
-- | the behaviour waits until a behaviour writes it, then is queued
-- | again, behind that one (the paper's `whenever`).
instance instanceWaiterCown : {e : HFunctor}
                            -> {a : Type}
                            -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
                            -> Waiter
                              e
                              (Cown a)
                              (\s -> a)
                              (\x -> x)
                              (\r -> Unit)
instanceWaiterCown =
  { heldWhere = \c written read p body ->
        behaviourWhere
          c
          written
          read
          (Just (\v -> p (s := Unit) v))
          (\v -> bind (body (s := Unit) v) (\q -> pure (snd q)))
  }

-- | ... on two cowns: let go of both, and waiting on those `p` reads.
instance instanceWaiterCowns : {e : HFunctor}
                             -> {a b : Type}
                             -> {{cw : Cowns (Cown a, Cown b)}}
                             -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
                             -> Waiter
                               e
                               (Cown a, Cown b)
                               (\s -> cw.Contents)
                               (\x -> x)
                               (\r -> Unit)
instanceWaiterCowns =
  { heldWhere = \c written read p body ->
        behaviourWhere
          c
          (cw := cw)
          written
          read
          (Just (\v -> p (s := Unit) v))
          (\v -> bind (body (s := Unit) v) (\q -> pure (snd q)))
  }

-- | ... on more.
instance instanceWaiterCownsMore : {e : HFunctor}
                                 -> {a d f : Type}
                                 -> {{cw : Cowns (Cown a, (d, f))}}
                                 -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
                                 -> Waiter
                                   e
                                   (Cown a, (d, f))
                                   (\s -> cw.Contents)
                                   (\x -> x)
                                   (\r -> Unit)
instanceWaiterCownsMore =
  { heldWhere = \c written read p body ->
        behaviourWhere
          c
          (cw := cw)
          written
          read
          (Just (\v -> p (s := Unit) v))
          (\v -> bind (body (s := Unit) v) (\q -> pure (snd q)))
  }

-- Awaiting the world
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (a behaviour on no cown, spawned: `body` its work)
alone : {e : HFunctor} -> {{bc : boc <: e}} -> Co e Unit -> Co e Unit
alone body =
  op
    (bc
       (When
          (\r use -> use Unit Unit (MkBehaviour MkUnit Nothing 0 0))
          (ran (\(u : Unit) -> bind body (\v -> pure u)))
          MkUnit))
    val

-- | What a `with` awaits, `r := BOC.await act` or `r := BOC.awaitFirst
-- | a b`: an act of the world `w`, the `with`'s, or two raced, coming to
-- | an `a`, outside every behaviour, the `with`'s body a behaviour once
-- | it has.
data Await (w : HFunctor) a
  = MkAwait (Co w a)
  | Raced (Co w a) (Co w a)

-- | `act` awaited by a `with`: `with r := BOC.await act do ...`.
await : {w : HFunctor} -> {a : Type} -> Co w a -> Await w a
await act = MkAwait act

-- (`c` run where the act of the race `tn` whose value this is came to
-- it first: the other given up then)
firstThen : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
          -> Turn
          -> Co e Unit
          -> Co e Unit
firstThen tn c =
  bind (op (bc (Won tn (\b -> b))) val) (\w -> if w then c else pure ())

-- (the act awaited, and `k` given its value once it has come to it:
-- both run outside every behaviour, `k`, as the act's rest, spawning
-- what is to come after it; the act put in the world of the `with`)
awaitedThen : {e : HFunctor}
            -> {a : Type}
            -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
            -> Await e a
            -> (a -> Co e Unit)
            -> Co e Unit
awaitedThen (MkAwait act) k = op (bc (Awaits (\tn -> bind act k) MkUnit)) val
awaitedThen (Raced l r) k =
  op
    (bc
       (Races
          (\tn -> bind l (\x -> firstThen tn (k x)))
          (\tn -> bind r (\y -> firstThen tn (k y)))
          MkUnit))
    val

-- | `a` and `b` awaited by a `with`, raced: `with r := BOC.awaitFirst a
-- | b do ...`, `r` `Left` what `a` came to or `Right` what `b` did,
-- | whichever came to its value first.  Both are started together; the
-- | first to come to its value gives up the other, and the body is a
-- | behaviour on that one's value alone.  A time limit is a race with a
-- | sleep.
awaitFirst : {w : HFunctor}
           -> {a b : Type}
           -> Co w a
           -> Co w b
           -> Await w (Either a b)
awaitFirst l r =
  Raced (bind l (\x -> pure (Left x))) (bind r (\y -> pure (Right y)))

-- | A `with` of an act awaited, `with r := BOC.await act do ...`: `r`
-- | what the act comes to; the `with` comes to nothing at once.
instance instanceBinderAwait : {w : HFunctor}
                             -> {a : Type}
                             -> Binder (Await w a)
instanceBinderAwait = { Contents = \x s -> a, Result = \r -> Unit }

-- | A `with` of cowns and an act awaited, the act the last binding
-- | (`with x := c` and `r := BOC.await act` under it): `x` what `c`, a
-- | cown or a tuple of them, holds, and `r` what the act comes to.
instance instanceBinderCownsAwait : {c : Type}
                                  -> {w : HFunctor}
                                  -> {a : Type}
                                  -> {{cw : Cowns c}}
                                  -> Binder (c, Await w a)
instanceBinderCownsAwait =
  { Contents = \x s -> (cw.Contents, a), Result = \r -> Unit }

-- | An act awaited by a `with`: the act run outside every behaviour,
-- | and once it has come to its value, the body a behaviour on no cown,
-- | given it.  The one awaiting goes on at once.
instance instanceHolderAwait : {e : HFunctor}
                             -> {a : Type}
                             -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
                             -> Holder
                               e
                               (Await e a)
                               (\s -> a)
                               (\x -> x)
                               (\r -> Unit)
instanceHolderAwait =
  { held = \aw written body ->
        awaitedThen
          aw
          (\v -> alone (bind (body (s := Unit) v) (\p -> pure MkUnit)))
  }

-- | ... and with cowns: once the act has come to its value, the body a
-- | behaviour on them, spawned then -- so behaviours on a cown that
-- | await acts are in the order their acts came to their values -- its
-- | names set what they hold after it, as a `with` of the cowns alone.
instance instanceHolderCownsAwait : {e : HFunctor}
                                  -> {c a : Type}
                                  -> {{cw : Cowns c}}
                                  -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
                                  -> Holder
                                    e
                                    (c, Await e a)
                                    (\s -> (cw.Contents, a))
                                    (\x -> x)
                                    (\r -> Unit)
instanceHolderCownsAwait =
  { held = \p written body ->
        awaitedThen
          (snd p)
          (\v ->
             behaviour
               (fst p)
               (cw := cw)
               [firstFlag written]
               (\t ->
                  bind (body (s := Unit) (t, v)) (\q -> pure (fst (snd q)))))
  }

-- Work spread
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (how many values are still to come, and those come, each in its place)
type Gathered b = (Int, Array (Maybe b))

-- (the values come, in their order: each place has one by then)
gatheredList : {b : Type} -> Array (Maybe b) -> List b
gatheredList got =
  Array.foldr
    (\m acc -> case m of
     Just y -> y :: acc
     Nothing -> acc)
    Nil
    got

-- (`f` of the `i`th of the list and those after it, each by a behaviour
-- on a cown of its own, which puts it in its place in `into`'s, by a
-- behaviour there; the last of them gives `k` the list)
spreadFrom : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {a b : Type}
           -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
           -> (a -> b)
           -> Cown (Gathered b)
           -> (List b -> Co e Unit)
           -> Int
           -> List a
           -> Co e Unit
spreadFrom f into k i [] = pure ()
spreadFrom f into k i (x :: rest) = do
  own <- newCown x
  when own (\v -> do
    let y = f v
    when into (\(left, got) -> do
      let now = Array.set got i (Just y)
      if left == 1 then k (gatheredList now) else pure ()
      pure (left - 1, now))
    pure v)
  spreadFrom f into k (i + 1) rest

-- | `f` of each of `xs`, each computed by a behaviour of its own, on a
-- | cown of its own, so that they run at once, on as many threads as
-- | the pool has; then `k` given what they came to, in the order of
-- | `xs`, by a behaviour once every one is done.  This one goes on at
-- | once.  Each value is put in its place by a behaviour on one cown
-- | the values gather in, which takes no longer than a write: the work
-- | is `f`'s, which is the behaviour's of its element alone.  So an `f`
-- | of little work is better given a piece of the list each (`xs` a
-- | list of lists).  Where an `f` fails, the failure is said, and `k`
-- | is never run.
parMap : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {a b : Type}
       -> {{bc : boc <: e}}
       -> (a -> b)
       -> List a
       -> (List b -> Co e Unit)
       -> Co e Unit
parMap f [] k = alone (k Nil)
parMap f xs k = do
  let n = List.length xs
  into <- newCown (n, Array.replicate n (Nothing : Maybe b))
  spreadFrom f into k 0 xs
